## 8. Side effect found, isolated, and *not* a cause of any of the 48

`reference/rust/gen/boring/value_type_consumer.rs` (and its f32 twin and their
test files) changes HEAD → our change set:

```
HEAD  : pub fn value_type_consumer_rendered_id() -> Result<UString, ValueError> {
            let id = FontFaceId::new(UStr::new(&[102,97,99,101]))?;
            return Ok(UString::from((id).to_string().as_str()));
OURS+RES / DIRTY (byte-identical):
        pub fn value_type_consumer_rendered_id() -> UString {
            let id = FontFaceId::new(UStr::new(&[102,97,99,101])).unwrap();
            return UString::from((id).to_string().as_str());
```

`reference/rust/gen/tests/value_type_tests.rs` loses its `.unwrap()` to match.
This is **error-propagation removal**: a recoverable `Result` + `?` path becomes
a non-`Result` function with a panic path. It is in a different domain from
PIT-248's stated intent (exception/fault payload-variant registration) and looks
unnoticed.

Attribution: `HEAD` produces the `Result` form, `OURS+RES` (= HEAD + our 10
paths + the required `RustEmissionState.hx`) produces the `.unwrap()` form
**byte-identically to DIRTY**. Since the only code-generation input we changed
is `Compiler.hx` (the hxml/allowlist/guard/package.json/NumberClassify changes
cannot alter Rust codegen), this is **our `Compiler.hx` revision
`57ee49976511892c`**, not the other in-flight Rust files. Note this narrows the
sibling seat's finding: an earlier "as-reviewed" `Compiler.hx` revision
(`24f79c27…`) emits the `Result` form, so the responsible hunk is one that
`57ee4997` has and `24f79c27` does not. I did not bisect further, and **I did not
change anything**.

Impact on this task: **zero**. No bun-collected test references
`value_type_consumer` or `value_type_tests` (0 hits over all 54 `*.test.ts`), and
both `value_type_ops.rs` and the value-type assertion path are byte-identical
across all three trees. So it causes no Stage-4 failure name — it is an
*uncovered* risk, not a cause.

